From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 13:50:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: rndis: reopen the data path and indicate media
 connect on INIT

The TP26 BIOS's UEFI RNDIS driver never sends OID_GEN_CURRENT_PACKET_FILTER.
In stock f_rndis/rndis that single OID is the only thing that (a) starts the
data path and (b) tells the driver the link is up, so the Redfish Host
Interface never comes alive during POST.  Patch 0022 dealt with the outgoing
cdc_filter; this patch deals with the two remaining consequences, both of which
were confirmed on hardware with dynamic_debug on the whole gadget stack.

1. netif carrier / TX queue

   rndis_msg_parser() handles RNDIS_MSG_HALT with

	params->state = RNDIS_UNINITIALIZED;
	if (params->dev) {
		netif_carrier_off(params->dev);
		netif_stop_queue(params->dev);
	}

   and the only code that ever undoes it is the
   RNDIS_OID_GEN_CURRENT_PACKET_FILTER case in gen_ndis_set_resp().  The BIOS
   does RESET/INIT/HALT/INIT twice and then stops, so the gadget is left
   INITIALIZED but with the carrier down and the queue stopped for the whole of
   POST.  Measured over a full POST:

	usb0 carrier=0 operstate=down    (313.99s .. 471.8s uptime)
	RX 22 packets (the BIOS's ARPs)  TX 6 packets

   With the carrier down, linkwatch_do_dev() calls dev_deactivate(), so the ARP
   replies the BIOS is waiting for are dropped before they ever reach
   eth_start_xmit().  The carrier only came up at 471.8s, when the host OS
   booted and its rndis_host driver set the packet filter.

   INIT is the point at which the endpoints are usable again, so re-enable the
   netdev there rather than waiting for an OID that this host never sends.

2. RNDIS_STATUS_MEDIA_CONNECT

   rndis_indicate_status_msg() returns -ENOTSUPP while the state is
   RNDIS_UNINITIALIZED, and f_rndis emits its only rndis_signal_connect() from
   rndis_open(), which gether_connect() calls at SET_CONFIGURATION -- i.e.
   strictly before the host's INIT.  The one media-connect indication is
   therefore generated exactly when it is guaranteed to be discarded, and none
   is ever generated afterwards.  A host that waits for a link-up indication
   before querying anything sees silence, which is what the trace shows: after
   the final INIT_CMPLT the BIOS sends nothing at all for the remaining 138 s
   of POST.

   Emit the indication once the state machine is initialized and the medium is
   connected.  It is queued after the INIT_CMPLT, and f_rndis's notify_count
   logic already resends RESPONSE_AVAILABLE until the queue drains, so the host
   collects the two responses in order.

Linux and Windows hosts are unaffected: they set the packet filter as before
(which re-runs netif_carrier_on()) and their RNDIS drivers ignore a redundant
INDICATE_STATUS.

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>

3. stale responses across a HALT

   RNDIS_MSG_HALT did not drain the response queue (RNDIS_MSG_RESET does).
   That is latent upstream, where nothing is ever queued unsolicited, but the
   indication added by (2) makes it reachable: the BIOS reads INIT_CMPLT and
   HALTs without collecting the trailing indication, so the next session's
   GET_ENCAPSULATED_RESPONSE is served the stale 20-byte indication where its
   52-byte INIT_CMPLT should be.  Confirmed on hardware -- the ep0 trace showed
   the BIOS reading l52 then l20 after every second INIT.  Drain on HALT too.

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/rndis.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/rndis.c
@@ -821,13 +821,49 @@
 
 	/* For USB: responses may take up to 10 seconds */
 	switch (MsgType) {
-	case RNDIS_MSG_INIT:
+	case RNDIS_MSG_INIT: {
+		int retval;
+
 		pr_debug("%s: RNDIS_MSG_INIT\n",
 			__func__);
 		params->state = RNDIS_INITIALIZED;
-		return rndis_init_response(params, (rndis_init_msg_type *)buf);
+		retval = rndis_init_response(params,
+					     (rndis_init_msg_type *)buf);
+		if (retval < 0)
+			return retval;
+
+		/*
+		 * A preceding RNDIS_MSG_HALT took the carrier down and stopped
+		 * the queue, and only RNDIS_OID_GEN_CURRENT_PACKET_FILTER puts
+		 * them back.  The TP26 UEFI RNDIS driver never sends that OID,
+		 * so bring the netdev back up here -- the endpoints are usable
+		 * again as of INIT.  Companion to the cdc_filter default in
+		 * f_rndis.c.
+		 */
+		if (params->dev) {
+			netif_carrier_on(params->dev);
+			if (netif_running(params->dev))
+				netif_wake_queue(params->dev);
+		}
+
+		/*
+		 * f_rndis signals connect from rndis_open(), which runs at
+		 * SET_CONFIGURATION -- before this INIT -- so that indication
+		 * is dropped by the RNDIS_UNINITIALIZED check in
+		 * rndis_indicate_status_msg().  Re-announce it now that the
+		 * state machine can carry it, for hosts that wait for link-up
+		 * before they query anything.
+		 */
+		if (params->media_state == RNDIS_MEDIA_STATE_CONNECTED)
+			rndis_indicate_status_msg(params,
+						  RNDIS_STATUS_MEDIA_CONNECT);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	case RNDIS_MSG_HALT: {
+		u8 *xbuf;
+		u32 xlen;
 
-	case RNDIS_MSG_HALT:
 		pr_debug("%s: RNDIS_MSG_HALT\n",
 			__func__);
 		params->state = RNDIS_UNINITIALIZED;
@@ -835,7 +871,22 @@
 			netif_carrier_off(params->dev);
 			netif_stop_queue(params->dev);
 		}
+
+		/*
+		 * Drain the response queue, exactly as RNDIS_MSG_RESET does.
+		 * HALT ends the session, so anything still queued belongs to
+		 * the old one; leaving it there makes the *next* session read
+		 * a stale response as the reply to its first command.  That is
+		 * latent upstream (nothing is queued unsolicited), but the
+		 * media-connect indication added above makes it reachable: the
+		 * host reads INIT_CMPLT, HALTs without collecting the trailing
+		 * indication, and the following INIT_CMPLT is then served one
+		 * response too late.  Observed on the TP26 BIOS.
+		 */
+		while ((xbuf = rndis_get_next_response(params, &xlen)))
+			rndis_free_response(params, xbuf);
 		return 0;
+	}
 
 	case RNDIS_MSG_QUERY:
 		return rndis_query_response(params,
